Verbatim 8-22: `/* Desktop/mobile trees coexist in the DOM; CSS swaps which one renders at
   the shared 767.98px breakpoint (AppShell/Sidebar/MobileHeader convention
   — see tokens.css "Mobile chrome layout"). */
.mobileOnly {
  display: none;
}

@media (width <= 767.98px) {
  .desktopOnly {
    display: none;
  }

  .mobileOnly {
    display: block;
  }
}`. Itself an instance of the LD-21 partial-override pattern — do NOT refactor in this run.

itemDetailPaths.desktopCssToday:
/Users/ozzy/App Development/Loombre/apps/web/src/components/detail/VersionCard.module.css:60-68 —

.path {
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-size: var(--mono-sm);
  letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
  white-space: nowrap;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

That is the ellipsis behavior LD-18 removes: `white-space: nowrap` + `overflow: hidden` + `text-overflow: ellipsis` truncates the TAIL of the path (the filename) on desktop. It is ALREADY monospace (`var(--font-mono)`) at `var(--mono-sm)` (9.5px) with `color: var(--color-text-muted)` — --color-text-muted on a --mono-* tier is the LD-14-conforming choice and must stay (ld14-mono-scale-conformance.test.ts enforces it repo-wide). `letter-spacing: 0.06em` is a literal here, not `var(--track-mono)` (siblings `.name`/`.defaultBadge` at 25/37 use the token; `.specs` at 55 uses the same 0.06em literal).

itemDetailPaths.mobileCssToday:
/Users/ozzy/App Development/Loombre/apps/web/src/components/detail/VersionCard.module.css:70-81 (the last block in the file) —

@media (width <= 767.98px) {
  .card {
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
  }

  .path {
    white-space: normal;
    word-break: break-all;
    line-height: var(--leading-dense);
  }
}

Mobile ALREADY implements LD-18's target convention (`word-break: break-all`, no clamp, no ellipsis) — but as a PARTIAL override: it resets `white-space` and adds `word-break`, and silently INHERITS the desktop `overflow: hidden` + `text-overflow: ellipsis` from lines 66-67 (harmless once wrapping, but exactly the LD-21 partial-override shape). `.card`'s mobile override at 71-74 re-declares `padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md)` identically to the desktop `.card` at line 6 — an existing no-op declaration.

glassCss.positionalAssumptions:
The rule itself (/Users/ozzy/App Development/Loombre/apps/web/src/styles/glass.css:21-35) declares NO position, inset, z-index, border-radius or box-shadow:

.glass {
  background: var(--chrome-bg);
  border: 1px solid var(--chrome-edge);
  backdrop-filter: blur(var(--chrome-blur));
}

/* Opaque elevated-surface fallback where backdrop-filter is unsupported
   (unchanged requirement). */
@supports not (
  (backdrop-filter: blur(1px)) or (-webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(1px))
) {
  .glass {
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }
}

The positional assumptions are IMPLICIT, carried by that one `backdrop-filter` line, and are these three:

1. THE BLUR MUST HAVE THE PAGE BEHIND IT TO SAMPLE. `backdrop-filter` samples the backdrop root. Any ancestor that itself sets backdrop-filter (or filter/opacity/transform/will-change) becomes a NEW backdrop root, and a `.glass` descendant then samples that ancestor's already-composited output instead of the page — the blur silently does nothing while the 86%-alpha fill stays translucent. Live ancestors that form such a root today: /Users/ozzy/App Development/Loombre/apps/web/src/components/shell/AppShell.module.css:43-56 (`.topbar` … `backdrop-filter: blur(14px)` at 54); /Users/ozzy/App Development/Loombre/apps/web/src/components/player/PlayerControls.module.css:54-72 (`.bottomBar` … `blur(18px)` at 66), 222-234 (`.pickerPopover` … `blur(18px)` at 229) and 28-42 (`.backButton` … `blur(6px)` at 38).

2. THE ELEMENT MUST SUPPLY ITS OWN POSITION AND STACKING. Nine of the ten consumers add `position: fixed|absolute|relative` plus their own z-index (fixed: MobileTabBar 40, MobileHeader 30, MiniPlayerBar 60, QueueDrawer 61; absolute: SceneBanner .backPill 3, Scrubber .hoverPreview [none], styleguide demo panel [none]; relative: AmbientHero .content 4, UnavailableScreen .panel 1). `.glass` contributes none of it. Note also that `backdrop-filter` makes the element a containing block for fixed/absolute descendants — so a `.glass` surface changes the positioning context of anything inside it.

3. IT DELIBERATELY OWNS NEITHER RADIUS NOR SHADOW — verbatim glass.css:11-14: "Consume via CSS Modules `composes: glass from global;` exactly as before; this recipe still deliberately does NOT set border-radius or box-shadow, so it never fights a component's own declarations in the cascade." Every consumer wanting a corner or shadow declares its own (setup .card 27-29, MiniPlayerBar 24-25, QueueDrawer 11-12, UnavailableScreen 25-26, styleguide 123-124, SceneBanner 97).

The two-surface reservation is already asserted in three places and the LD-20 comment must not contradict them: glass.css:16-18 (".glass is retained (not removed) — the mobile tab bar and now-playing bar (Wave 1+) both consume it, same as the desktop sidebar/topbar chrome and every modal/popover/sheet today."); /Users/ozzy/App Development/Loombre/apps/web/src/styles/tokens.css:221-226 ("Chrome scrim — replaces liquid glass (U8). Consumed ONLY via the shared `.glass` recipe, src/styles/glass.css — component CSS should not hand-roll backdrop-filter from these directly." then `--chrome-bg: rgb(18 20 25 / 86%); --chrome-blur: 20px; --chrome-edge: rgb(255 255 255 / 7%);`); /Users/ozzy/App Development/Loombre/design/phosphor/README.md:66-69 ("Keep `.glass` for the mobile tab bar and now-playing bar"). Note the tension the LD-20 comment must navigate honestly: the two SANCTIONED surfaces are MobileTabBar and MiniPlayerBar, but eight other files consume `.glass` today, and three shell/player files (AppShell.module.css:30-42, Sidebar.module.css:17, QuickSearch.module.css:11-18, PlayerControls.module.css:3-11/49-53/219-221, home/PosterCard.module.css:232-233) carry comments explaining why they DROPPED it.

playerControls.existingMediaQueries:
EXACTLY ONE media query touches PlayerControls, in PlayerControls.module.css:205-217. Verbatim, comment included:

/* Below the mobile breakpoint the transport cluster (skip/play/skip/mute
   + this 80px slider, ~260px) plus the right icon cluster no longer fit
   the bar's inner width (~282px at 380px viewport) — the fullscreen
   button spilled ~40px past the bar and 17px off the viewport entirely
   (2026-08-27 QA). The slider is the one drop that restores fit; the
   mute toggle stays, and phones control volume with hardware buttons.
   767.98px is the shared mobile-breakpoint literal (tokens.css "Mobile
   chrome layout"). */
@media (width <= 767.98px) {
  .volumeSlider {
    display: none;
  }
}

No other stylesheet in apps/web contains a media query targeting PlayerControls' classes (verified by grepping every PlayerControls reference across apps/web/src, docs/ and README.md — remaining hits are comments in NoticeOverlayStrip.module.css:5-6, Scrubber.module.css:14, Scrubber.tsx:27/45/128, VideoPlayer.tsx:87/212/351-354/2388/2546/2586/2602, Icon.tsx:24, and the three CSS-reading tests).

Two NON-media conditional blocks also exist in the file and must not be confused with media queries: PlayerControls.module.css:74-80 (`@supports not ((backdrop-filter: blur(1px)) or (-webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(1px))) { .bottomBar { background: var(--color-bg-raised); } }`) and 236-242 (same fallback for `.pickerPopover`).

Breakpoint syntax note: the JS form at PlayerControls.tsx:55 is `(max-width: 767.98px)` while every CSS block in the repo uses the range form `(width <= 767.98px)`. Same value, two syntaxes; the CSS-text-parsing test idiom (phosphor-mobile-css.test.ts:27) matches only the range form.

stylelint.currentConfig:
{
  "extends": ["stylelint-config-standard"],
  "rules": {
    "custom-property-pattern": null,
    "selector-class-pattern": null,
    "import-notation": null,
    "property-no-unknown": [
      true,
      { "ignoreProperties": ["composes"] }
    ],
    "property-no-vendor-prefix": [
      true,
      { "ignoreProperties": ["backdrop-filter"] }
    ],
    "declaration-property-value-allowed-list": {
      "/^border-radius$/": [
        "/^var\\(--radius-(pill|lg|md|sm|full)\\)$/",
        "/^var\\(--radius-(pill|lg|md|sm|full)\\) var\\(--radius-(pill|lg|md|sm|full)\\)( var\\(--radius-(pill|lg|md|sm|full)\\) var\\(--radius-(pill|lg|md|sm|full)\\))?$/",
        "0",
        "inherit"
      ],
      "/^border-(top|bottom)-(left|right)-radius$/": [
        "/^var\\(--radius-(pill|lg|md|sm|full)\\)$/",
        "0",
        "inherit"
      ]
    }
  }
}

No `plugins` key. No `overrides`. No `ignoreFiles`. The one bespoke rule today is the U5 radius law (`declaration-property-value-allowed-list`), which is the precedent for adding another config-level rule.
